  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nAfternoon high temperatures have come down marginally and are \nexpected to drop a couple more degrees over the coming days. \nNonetheless, the Desert Southwest high is still in place and we \nare running hotter than normal by about 5 degrees, leaving most\nvalley locations with triple-digit highs throughout the week. Some\nareas may still break high temperature records. Overnight lows \nlook to fair a little better though with most of the region \napproaching near-normal lows through the first half of the week\nbefore we see nighttime temperatures warm back up in the latter\nhalf of the week. This extended period of hotter-than-normal \ntemperatures paired with a drier atmosphere is prompting another\nround of fire weather concerns though. Today, locally strong \nwinds near the Four Corners and relative humidities potentially \nin the single digits have prompted Red Flag Warnings. These fire\nweather concerns shift to the north tomorrow as a broad, yet \nshallow trough drops southwards across the northern Rockies. \nWith strengthening flow overhead tomorrow afternoon as a result,\nRed Flag Warnings have been issued for some northwestern \nColorado zones.\n\nThere is some uncertainty with both today and tomorrow's \ncritical fire weather conditions though, namely in the \nanticipated wind speeds. NBM gusts may be overdone slightly in \nthe Four Corners today, and we have yet to see widespread strong\ngusts forming in this area per surface observations. \nRegardless, some localized areas are starting to see wind speeds\nperking up, and the relative humidities are already well below \ncritical fire weather thresholds. Tomorrow, hi-res model \nguidance has yet to pinpoint corridors of stronger gusts. Higher\nconfidence areas to exceed 25 mph gusts reside over the high \nterrain of the Flat Tops and Park Range. However, relative \nhumidities over the higher elevations are not expected to fall \ninto critical fire weather thresholds. Regardless, we are \nanticipating scattered areas of Red Flag conditions in these \nzones and feel confidence is high enough to support the fire \nweather highlights. Stay tuned over the next 24 hours for \nupdates.",
